BEGIN:VEVENT
SUMMARY:An Evening with Amazonian Leader Ramiro Vargas
DTSTART;TZID=America/Los_Angeles:20260929T180000
DTEND;TZID=America/Los_Angeles:20260929T193000
UID:260916SOwded6pckaIeY1_XDkx1A
DESCRIPTION:First contacted by the Western world in the 1960’s\, the Ach
 uar people of the Ecuadorian Amazon have spent the intervening decades pro
 tecting the rainforest from oil drilling\, gold mining\, and logging. The 
 Achuar recognize themselves to be the forest and understand that the prote
 ction of the jungle\, their culture and their communities are inseparable.
 \n\nCome spend an evening with Achuar guide and leader of his community\, 
 Ramiro Vargas\, lovingly known as Chumpi\, in conversation about the Achua
 r way of life and what it is to live at one with nature. Chumpi will speak
  about how\, despite increasing threats of environmental destruction and c
 ultural imperialism\, they are nurturing and sustaining their language\, l
 ifeways\, and traditions.\n\nCommunity cohesion and capacity for intergene
 rational exchange are critical for the protection of territory\, tradition
 al knowledge and rights. Chumpi will share the simple but innovative ways 
 of fostering unity in the Achuar territories.
LOCATION:ST. STEPHEN'S EPISCOPAL CHURCH
URL:https://www.pressdemocrat.com/events/#/details/an-evening-with-amazoni
 an-leader-ramiro-vargas/19933588/2026-09-29T18
X-SOURCE:Press Democrat
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
SUMMARY:Tim Kliphuis & Jimmy
DTSTART;TZID=America/Los_Angeles:20260929T190000
DTEND;TZID=America/Los_Angeles:20260929T190000
UID:260910K7ZOKxQlF0SH0J4T2kh_DQ
DESCRIPTION:Native to Northern California\, Jimmy Grant  grew up in a hous
 ehold full of rich musical influences. While his early  influences also in
 cluded Russian Folk\, Balkan\, traditional Hungarian\,  Bluegrass\, Celtic
  and Classical\,  from a young age he gravitated towards the music of Djan
 go Reinhardt.  He plays a blend of musics from all over the world and his 
 own compositions.    He has studied with  acclaimed guitarists Jim Nichols
  and Howard Alden\, and in addition to  Django Reinhardt\, his other major
  musical influences are Bach\, Kovacs  Andor\, Roby Lakatos and Angelo Deb
 arre. He has performed  with artists such as David Grisman\, Paulus Schaef
 er \,Tim Kliphuis\,  Gonzalo Bergara\, Ludovic Beier\, Velvet Caravan\, Rh
 ythm Future Quartet\,  Hot Club of San Francisco and more\, and has played
  at the Savannah Musci  Festival\, Los Angeles Django Reinhardt Festival\,
   Nor-Cal Jazz Fest\, Djangofest Northwest and The New York Hot Jazz  Fest
 ival.    He has recorded two albums under his own name\, and performed on 
 several other albums. Jimmy's original song “Insomnia” was a finalist 
 in the John Lennon songwriting competition\\(in the jazz category\\). As J
 immy puts  it\, “We believe that Jazz\, and the music of Django\, is a p
 iece of history worth preserving.” \n\n In addition to performing\, Jimm
 y teaches private  students and has been on the faculty of the Django in J
 une Festival in  Massachusetts\, and also is the artistic director of the 
 Django Reinhardt  Association.  Jimmy also writes for “Gypsy Jazz Secret
 s Magazine”.   He has been featured in Relix magazine and Acoustic Guita
 r magazine.
LOCATION:The Lost Church
URL:https://www.bandsintown.com/e/108725663?app_id=pkvts0on0m&came_from=28
 7&utm_medium=api&utm_source=partner_api&utm_campaign=event
X-SOURCE:Press Democrat
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
SUMMARY:Henry Frank: J-80928: A Human Experience Exhibition
DTSTART;TZID=America/Los_Angeles:20260929T100000
DTEND;TZID=America/Los_Angeles:20260929T170000
UID:2609064yK1zLDRXUGhoa_kVjAmmQ
DESCRIPTION:The San Geronimo Valley Community Center is proud to present f
 eatured September artist Henry Frank in the Del Mue Galleries this Septem
 ber. There will be a reception for the artist as part of the International
  Day of Peace Festival Finale on September 20 from 2 – 6 pm.\n\nJ-80928:
  A Human Experience Exhibition explores Henry Frank’s transformative jo
 urney through nearly three decades of incarceration in the California Depa
 rtment of Corrections. Through deeply personal artwork\, the exhibition re
 flects on trauma\, friendship\, identity\, hope\, and personal growth whil
 e inviting viewers to recognize the humanity and capacity for change in pe
 ople who are incarcerated.\n\nARTISTS STATEMENT J-80928: A Human Experienc
 e Exhibition explores my personal journey serving a 29-year-to-life senten
 ce in the California Department of Corrections as an Indigenous person\, a
 n artist\, and a human being. I was incarcerated from February 15\, 1994\,
  to April 19\, 2013\, and during much of that time\, I lived hour to hour.
  For the first 14 years\, my mantras were: expect the worst and hope for t
 he best\, trust no one except myself\, and fear is a state of mind. While 
 many experiences were deeply traumatic\, there were also moments of humani
 ty\, compassion\, joy\, and empathy that transformed how I saw myself and 
 the world around me.\n\nThe last six years of my incarceration became the 
 most transformative period of my life. I began to believe that I would not
  die in prison. I found hope\, discovered the importance of serving other
 s\, began to see myself as a serious artist\, and gained a deeper understa
 nding of my place in the world. My mantras evolved into: expect the best a
 nd be prepared for anything less\, trust everyone until they give me a rea
 son not to\, and happiness is a choice. During this time\, I learned to lo
 ve and respect myself\, to be accountable\, to live in the present\, to be
  a true friend\, and to serve others with intention and compassion.\n\nThi
 s exhibition is an oeuvre dedicated to the experiences\, emotional trauma\
 , friendships\, and reclamation of identity that shaped me throughout my i
 ncarceration. Through this body of work\, I hope people can connect with p
 arts of my journey and recognize that I am not so different from them. Mor
 e importantly\, I want to humanize those who are currently incarcerated an
 d raise awareness that people are capable of change. They can become contr
 ibuting members of their communities. They have families\, hobbies\, hopes
 \, dreams\, and the capacity for growth and transformation.\n\nI have been
  working toward completing this body of work and plan to have the exhibiti
 on finished completely by the end of this year.\n\nThis exhibition is part
 ially funded by an Artist Fellowship Grant from the Art for Justice Fund. 
 I am grateful for their support and patience for this exhibition to evolve
  into its fullest potential.\n\nHuman Being\n\nHenry Edward Frank\n\nYurok
  and Kashaya Pomo
LOCATION:San Geronimo Valley Community Center
URL:https://sangeronimovalleycommunitycenter.eventcalendarapp.com/henry-fr
 ank-j-80928-a-human-experience-exhibition
X-SOURCE:Press Democrat
END:VEVENT
