DTSTART;TZID=America/Los_Angeles:20261002T193000
DTEND;TZID=America/Los_Angeles:20261002T221500
UID:260915hF4DOiNaNEubVkE9eyQARg
DESCRIPTION:Emilia by Morgan Lloyd Malcolm is a passionate\, feminist rei
 magining of the life of 17th-century poet and musician Emilia Bassano. The
  play\, performed by an all-female cast\, delves into her journey from a p
 rivileged childhood\, to becoming a trailblazing poet\, and a woman fighti
 ng for her say in a male-dominated world.
LOCATION:SRJC Burbank Auditorium
URL:https://srjc.universitytickets.com/
X-SOURCE:Press Democrat
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
SUMMARY:Grand Bazaar of Petaluma
DTSTART;TZID=America/Los_Angeles:20261002T120000
DTEND;TZID=America/Los_Angeles:20261002T170000
UID:260912z3lTVBe-L0m5AsPeziKoWQ
DESCRIPTION:Join us for an lively\, in-person marketplace bursting with un
 ique finds from over 125 talented artists\, crafters\, and vendors\, showc
 asing a exciting mix of creative treasures — from festive holiday décor
  and gifts to rare collectibles and one-of-a-kind art. Every day brings so
 mething new to explore!\n\nBrowse an incredible selection of products\, in
 cluding jewelry\, clothing\, art\, woodworking\, glasswork\, pottery\, kid
 s’ toys\, miniatures\, candles\, soaps\, and much more. Discover beautif
 ul vintage and handmade pieces\, plus practical essentials like pet access
 ories and gift cards.  Featuring tasty treats from Sax's Joint\, Sabor\, 
 Pupusas la Bendicion\, The Chicks with the Lemonade\, and more. Santa will
  visit to get an early look at those Wish Lists!\n\nProceeds from this eve
 nt benefit Micah’s Hugs\, an inspiring local nonprofit dedicated to educ
 ating youth about the dangers of substance abuse and fentanyl poisoning.\n
 \nOur 2025 Grand Bazaar welcomed more than 3\,000 community members and ra
 ised over $20\,000 to support this life-saving mission. Join us this year 
 for an even bigger\, better\, and more spacious event. Together\, we can m
 ake a meaningful difference for our youth—and you may even learn how to 
 save a life.
LOCATION:Petaluma Fairgrounds
URL:https://giving4life.org/2026-grand-bazaar/
X-SOURCE:Press Democrat
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
SUMMARY:Dear Evan Hansen
DTSTART;TZID=America/Los_Angeles:20261002T193000
DTEND;TZID=America/Los_Angeles:20261002T220000
UID:260911PNvXV9pT_02DVR61vl-C1Q
DESCRIPTION:Poignant\, raw\, and of-the-moment\, Dear Evan Hansen pulls ba
 ck the curtain on the profound desire for human connection in the digital 
 age\, offering an empathetic look at the alienation of young people naviga
 ting modern social pressures.\n\nThe story follows Evan\, a high school se
 nior struggling with severe social anxiety who writes motivational letters
  to himself as a therapeutic exercise. When a troubled classmate dies by s
 uicide with one of Evan’s letters in his pocket\, the boy's grieving par
 ents mistakenly believe Evan was his secret best friend. Desperate to comf
 ort them\, Evan plays along\, spiraling into a web of fabricated emails an
 d internet fame before he must ultimately face the truth.\n\nPowered by a 
 critically acclaimed pop-rock score and the sweeping anthems\, Dear Even H
 ansen is a powerful reminder that we are never truly alone. Spreckels is p
 roud to present the North Bay premiere of this six-time Tony Award winning
  musical!
LOCATION:Spreckels Center for the Performing Arts
URL:https://spreckelsonline.com/show/2026-27-season/dear-evan-hansen/
X-SOURCE:Press Democrat
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
SUMMARY:Bye Bye Birdie
DTSTART;TZID=America/Los_Angeles:20261002T193000
DTEND;TZID=America/Los_Angeles:20261002T213000
UID:260910LTDzFKe4S06IIMMe7CG-5w
DESCRIPTION:A joyful\, high-energy musical comedy packed with unforgettabl
 e songs\, humor\, and heart\, "Bye Bye Birdie" is a celebration of youth\,
  fame\, and the delightful chaos that happens when pop culture collides wi
 th small-town life.\n\nFrom the high-energy “The Telephone Hour” to th
 e show-stopping “A Lot of Livin’ to Do\,” the heartfelt “How Lovel
 y to Be a Woman\,” and the iconic “Put On a Happy Face\,” this produ
 ction is filled with classic musical theater moments the whole family can 
 enjoy.\n\nCinnabar Theater's production is directed by Zachary Hasbany\, w
 ith book by Michael Stewart\, music by Charles Strouse\, and lyrics by Lee
  Adams\, originally produced by Edward Padula. The show is presented throu
 gh special arrangement with Concord Theatricals.\n\n**Show details**\n\nCr
 eated by: Book by Michael Stewart\, music by Charles Strouse\, lyrics by L
 ee Adams\n\nDirector: Zachary Hasbany\n\nLocation: Sonoma State University
  – Warren Theater\, Ives Hall\, 1801 East Cotati Ave\, Rohnert Park\, CA
  94928\n\nPerformance dates: September 18 – October 4\, 2026\; Fridays &
  Saturdays 7:30pm\, Sundays 2:00pm\n\nEstimated run time: 2 hours\, with o
 ne 10-minute intermission\n\nRecommended for families / all ages\n\n**ASL-
 Interpreted Performance**\n\nSunday\, September 27 at 2:00 PM\n\nThis perf
 ormance will feature American Sign Language (ASL) interpretation. Patrons 
 who would like seating with a clear view of the ASL interpreter can contac
 t the Box Office at (707) 763-8920 or boxoffice@cinnabartheater.org.\n\nTi
 ckets: https://ci.ovationtix.com/35211
LOCATION:Sonoma State University - Warren Auditorium
URL:https://cinnabartheater.org/show/byebyebirdie/
X-SOURCE:Press Democrat
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
SUMMARY:Best of San Francisco Stand-Up Comedy 2026
DTSTART;TZID=America/Los_Angeles:20261002T193000
DTEND;TZID=America/Los_Angeles:20261002T193000
UID:260909Ow9ZYgD6FUSeJclS0BUEcg
DESCRIPTION:San Francisco has played a crucial role in developing some of 
 the most notable acts in comedy history. From Robin Williams and Dana Carv
 ey to Ali Wong and Moshe Kasher\, San Francisco has shaped stand-up comedy
 ’s impact on American culture since the days of Lenny Bruce. Now\, a new
  generation of comedic talent is set to take the stage at the Raven on Oct
 ober 2nd\, so set your expectations extremely high. One of the longest run
 ning independent shows in America\, the Best of San Francisco Stand-Up Com
 edy is a comedy tour de force from some of the hottest up and coming acts 
 in the Bay Area.\n\n**October 2nd\, 7:30PM**\n\n**Gold (Front 6 rows):** $
 39 **General:** $29 (Includes fees.)
LOCATION:The Raven Performing Arts Theater
URL:https://raventheater.org/events/best-of-sf-stand-up2026/
X-SOURCE:Press Democrat
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
SUMMARY:Reception and Art Sale: California Native Creations Art Show
DTSTART;TZID=America/Los_Angeles:20261002T170000
DTEND;TZID=America/Los_Angeles:20261002T190000
UID:26090907xmaZZcwkWBuNMT1myvsg
DESCRIPTION:**September is the 17th Annual Pomo Honoring Month. On Friday\
 , Oct. 2\, from 5-7 p.m.\, the public is invited to the 6th California Nat
 ive Creations Art Show artists' reception and art sale\, which features th
 e Tri-County Pomo Dancers.**\n\nThe established and up-and-coming native a
 rtists create with digital media\, paint and traditional materials in thei
 r art\, weaving\, regalia\, beadwork\, jewelry\, baskets and quilts. The 
 artists showing or selling work include:\n\n·         Meyo Marruf
 o\, painter\, guest curator for the de Young Museum and the Sonoma Valley 
 Art Museum\,\n\n·         Buffie Campbell\, Vice Chair\, Sherwood
  Valley Rancheria\, cultural teacher and master basket maker\,\n\n·   
       Henry Edward Frank\, *Yurok/Kashaya Pomo*\, who will show paint
 ings and work with Legos titled *Mind Prison\,*\n\n·         Digi
 tal artist Nicolas (Nico) Vargas\, *Kashia Band of Pomo Indians*\, whose a
 rt was featured at the 55th Annual Stanford Powwow\,\n\n\\* plus Gloria Ca
 rretero\; Rose Hammock\; Rudy Hammock\; Jocelyn Garcia\; Arella Ortega LaR
 ose\, *Kashia Band of Pomo Indians*\; Angelica Smith\,*Manchester Point Ar
 ena and Yokayo Rancheria*\; Maria Pasalo\; Michelle Napoli\; Martina Morga
 n\, *Kashia Band of Pomo Indians*\; Hands Lopez\; Shashoni Hanover-Redenb
 augh\; Katie Williams-Elliott\, Juan Partida\, and Mia Pasalo\, *Big Valle
 y Pomo Tribe*.\n\n“We are not just people of the past\,” says Rose Ham
 mock (*Pomo\, Wailacki\, & Maidu*\, *Tribal Member Round Valley Indian Tri
 bes)*\, community leader\, native culture teacher and Pomo Project Board M
 ember. “There is great importance in highlighting Native Artists in publ
 ic spaces. For so long\, our communities and their many talents have gone 
 unnoticed and unrecognized - today that narrative has shifted. Now there a
 re Native Art Shows\, Fashion Shows\, and Art Showcases highlighting art a
 nd basketry."\n\nSays digital artist and native dancer Nicholas Vargas\, 
 “To me\, my art is a living archive\, a digital space where I reclaim ou
 r narrative and honor the resilience of my people.”\n\nPainter and bead 
 worker Gloria Carretero (*Pinoleville Pomo Nation Tribe*) adds\, **“**I 
 wish to honor my ancestors with native-inspired art.”
LOCATION:Person Senior Wing\, Finley Community Center
URL:http://www.pomoproject.org/
X-SOURCE:Press Democrat
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
SUMMARY:Henry Frank: J-80928: A Human Experience Exhibition
DTSTART;TZID=America/Los_Angeles:20261002T100000
DTEND;TZID=America/Los_Angeles:20261002T170000
UID:260906VwGgM7InvUqzXLxoxfph8Q
DESCRIPTION:The San Geronimo Valley Community Center is proud to present f
 eatured September artist Henry Frank in the Del Mue Galleries this Septem
 ber. There will be a reception for the artist as part of the International
  Day of Peace Festival Finale on September 20 from 2 – 6 pm.\n\nJ-80928:
  A Human Experience Exhibition explores Henry Frank’s transformative jo
 urney through nearly three decades of incarceration in the California Depa
 rtment of Corrections. Through deeply personal artwork\, the exhibition re
 flects on trauma\, friendship\, identity\, hope\, and personal growth whil
 e inviting viewers to recognize the humanity and capacity for change in pe
 ople who are incarcerated.\n\nARTISTS STATEMENT J-80928: A Human Experienc
 e Exhibition explores my personal journey serving a 29-year-to-life senten
 ce in the California Department of Corrections as an Indigenous person\, a
 n artist\, and a human being. I was incarcerated from February 15\, 1994\,
  to April 19\, 2013\, and during much of that time\, I lived hour to hour.
  For the first 14 years\, my mantras were: expect the worst and hope for t
 he best\, trust no one except myself\, and fear is a state of mind. While 
 many experiences were deeply traumatic\, there were also moments of humani
 ty\, compassion\, joy\, and empathy that transformed how I saw myself and 
 the world around me.\n\nThe last six years of my incarceration became the 
 most transformative period of my life. I began to believe that I would not
  die in prison. I found hope\, discovered the importance of serving other
 s\, began to see myself as a serious artist\, and gained a deeper understa
 nding of my place in the world. My mantras evolved into: expect the best a
 nd be prepared for anything less\, trust everyone until they give me a rea
 son not to\, and happiness is a choice. During this time\, I learned to lo
 ve and respect myself\, to be accountable\, to live in the present\, to be
  a true friend\, and to serve others with intention and compassion.\n\nThi
 s exhibition is an oeuvre dedicated to the experiences\, emotional trauma\
 , friendships\, and reclamation of identity that shaped me throughout my i
 ncarceration. Through this body of work\, I hope people can connect with p
 arts of my journey and recognize that I am not so different from them. Mor
 e importantly\, I want to humanize those who are currently incarcerated an
 d raise awareness that people are capable of change. They can become contr
 ibuting members of their communities. They have families\, hobbies\, hopes
 \, dreams\, and the capacity for growth and transformation.\n\nI have been
  working toward completing this body of work and plan to have the exhibiti
 on finished completely by the end of this year.\n\nThis exhibition is part
 ially funded by an Artist Fellowship Grant from the Art for Justice Fund. 
 I am grateful for their support and patience for this exhibition to evolve
  into its fullest potential.\n\nHuman Being\n\nHenry Edward Frank\n\nYurok
  and Kashaya Pomo
LOCATION:San Geronimo Valley Community Center
URL:https://sangeronimovalleycommunitycenter.eventcalendarapp.com/henry-fr
 ank-j-80928-a-human-experience-exhibition
X-SOURCE:Press Democrat
END:VEVENT
